 "result": "I read all 20 modules in full, in order, without grep.\n\n**Functions that divide without checking for zero**\n\nEach module has one `share_nXX(total, parts)` helper that returns `total / parts`. In five modules it never checks `parts` first, so it raises `ZeroDivisionError` when `parts == 0`:\n\n| Module | Function |\n|---|---|\n| `calc/n02.py:4` | `share_n02` |\n| `calc/n07.py:4` | `share_n07` |\n| `calc/n11.py:4` | `share_n11` |\n| `calc/n15.py:4` | `share_n15` |\n| `calc/n19.py:4` | `share_n19` |\n\nThe other 15 `share_nXX` functions return 0 when `parts == 0`. Nothing else in these files divides: the many `step_*` functions only multiply, add, compare, take `abs`, `math.sqrt` and `round`.\n\n**Answers**\n\n- **(a)** `MAX_BATCH = 384` in `calc/n04.py:9`.\n- **(b)** `retry_policy()` is defined in `calc/n13.py:9`. It returns `min(attempt * 2, 20)`.\n- **(c)** In `calc/n17.py`, `close_books(rows)` calls `archive()` (line 13\u201314). `archive` itself is defined at line 9.\n\nOne small thing: each module's docstring is numbered 20 higher than its filename. For example, `n01.py` says `\"calc.m21\"` and `n20.py` says `\"calc.m40\"`. It doesn't affect how the code runs, but it could confuse someone looking up a module by its docstring.",
